🚀 Building a 3-Tier Serverless View Counter with S3, Lambda & DynamoDB

I recently built and deployed a custom real-time view counter for my personal website on AWS. While static sites don't need traditional servers, this project was a great exercise in implementing a 3-Tier Serverless Architecture.

Here is how the architecture is separated across the three tiers:

🛠️ The 3-Tier Stack:

Presentation Tier (Frontend): Static HTML/CSS/JS hosted on Amazon S3 serving the user interface and client-side fetch() scripts.

Logic Tier (Backend): An AWS Lambda Function URL (configured with GET and POST methods) handling business logic, preflight CORS, and HTTP responses.

Data Tier (Database): An Amazon DynamoDB table persisting the live state and view counts.

💡 Key Takeaways & Best Practices Learned:

Serverless 3-Tier Alignment: You don't need EC2 instances or dedicated VMs to follow standard 3-tier principles—separating presentation, execution, and state managed by AWS keeps the system modular and highly available.

Handling CORS Gracefully: Preflight OPTIONS requests are managed automatically by Lambda Function URL CORS settings once your code returns matching response headers.

Zero Overhead & High Scalability: Combining S3, pay-per-use Lambda, and DynamoDB means running costs stay near zero while seamlessly handling spikes in site traffic.

